$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):EXP_PROC
$DATUM General/Typical Procedure: Compound 19 (99 mg, 0.182 mmol) was dissolved
in hydrogen chloride solution in methanol (3 mL) at 0 °C. The mixture was
stirred at this temperature until the disappearance of the starting
material (about 15 min) and then condensed in vacuo to give the crude
ammonium salt. To this salt, HOBt (27 mg, 0.20 mmol, 1.1 equiv) and
Boc-N- Me-Ala-OH (37 mg, 0.182 mmol, 1.0 equiv) in CH2Cl2 (2 mL) at 0 °C
was added EDCI (38 mg, 0.20 mmol, 1.1 equiv) and then diisopropylethyl
amine was added dropwise to adjust the pH of the reaction mixture to 8
(about 180 μL). The mixture was stirred at room temperature over night
and the solvent was evaporated under reduced pressure, eluted with ethyl
acetate (10 mL), washed successively with saturated NaHCO3 solution,
saturated NH4Cl solution and brine, then dried over Na2SO4. The solvent
was evaporated under reduced pressure and the residue was purified by
chromatography (hexane/ethyl acetate 1:1) to give 105 mg of the coupling
product 19 as colorless oil. The above oil (60 mg) was dissolved in
hydrogen chloride solution in methanol (2 mL) at 0 °C. The mixture was
stirred at this temperature until the disappearance of the starting
material (about 15 min) and then condensed in vacuo to give the crude
ammonium salt. The crude product was purified by chromatography on a C18
reverse phase semi-preparative HPLC column and then lyophilized to give
compound 11 (51 mg, 91% yield, 3 steps). Gradient ran from 65% of solvent
A and 35% of solvent B to 40% of solvent A and 60% of solvent B in 20
min. The purity was confirmed by analytical HPLC to be over 99% The same
procedure for compound 11 was used for compound 10 except for a little
change described below. Before removing of the Boc protecting group, the
coupling product (50 mg) was dissolved in methanol (2 mL). To this
solution Pd/C (5 mg, 10wt%) was added and the mixture was stirred at room
temperature for 3 h under H2 atmosphere (1 atm). The reaction mixture was
filtrated through Celite and the filtrate was concentrated in vacuo. The
resulting residue was treated with hydrogen chloride solution in methanol
(2 mL) at 0 °C and workup as described for compound 11. The crude product
was purified by chromatography on a C18 reverse phase semi- preparative
HPLC column and then lyophilized to give compound 10 (40 mg, 85% yield, 4
steps). Gradient ran from 65% of solvent A and 35% of solvent B to 40% of
solvent A and 60% of solvent B in 20 min. The purity was confirmed by
analytical HPLC to be over 98%. 1H NMR (300 MHz, DMSO-d6) δ 8.78 (d, J=
7.8 Hz, 1H), 8.62-8.95 (br, 2H), 8.24 (d, J= 8.7 Hz, 1H), 7.05-7.33 (m,
9H), 4.93 (dd, J= 8.1, 12.0 Hz, 1H), 4.65-4.83 (m, 1H), 4.23 (dd, J= 8.1,
12.0 Hz, 1H), 4.07-4.18 (m, 1H), 3.78-3.94 (m, 1H), 2.65-2.87 (m, 3H),
2.57 (d, J= 9.9 Hz, 1H), 1.88-2.20 (m, 3H), 1.38-1.88 (m, 11H), 1.37 (d,
J= 6.9 Hz, 3H); 13C NMR (100 MHz, CDCl3) δ 180.6, 179.0, 177.6, 150.2,
147.5, 146.9, 138.5, 138.4, 138.3, 138.2, 136.6, 136.0, 135.6, 70.9,
69.8, 65.8, 59.8, 56.4, 54.0, 44.9, 44.5, 42.6, 40.7, 40.0, 39.0, 38.6,
34.8, 31.7, 30.1, 25.6; ESI-MS m/z 531.4 (M + H)+ , 553.4 (M + Na)+ ;
HRMS (MALDI) Calcd. for C32H43N4O3 (M + H)+ requires 531.3330, found
551.3334; Anal. (C32H42N4O3.HCl.CF3CO2H.1.5H2O): C, H, N.
